// HOSA Bowl lessons 6–10: current health topics, history of medicine, healthcare systems with careers and ethics,
// nutrition with safety and infection control, and math conversions with match-day strategy.
const RULES=['HOSA Bowl 2026–27 guidelines','https://hosa.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/HOSA-Bowl-26-27.pdf'];
const MAG=['NIH MedlinePlus Magazine','https://magazine.medlineplus.gov/'];
const NLM=['National Library of Medicine: History of Medicine','https://www.nlm.nih.gov/hmd/index.html'];
const MEDICARE=['Medicare.gov: Parts of Medicare','https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/medicare-basics/parts-of-medicare'];
const HIPAA=['HHS: summary of the HIPAA Privacy Rule','https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/laws-regulations/index.html'];
const ISO=['CDC: isolation precautions guideline','https://www.cdc.gov/infection-control/hcp/isolation-precautions/index.html'];
const DGA=['Dietary Guidelines for Americans','https://www.dietaryguidelines.gov/'];
const q=(p,a,b,c,d,e)=>[p,[a,b,c,d],e];
const match=(title,pairs)=>({t:'w',kind:'match',title,pairs});
const order=(title,steps)=>({t:'w',kind:'order',title,steps});
const sort=(title,buckets,items)=>({t:'w',kind:'sort',title,buckets,items});
const flip=(title,cards)=>({t:'w',kind:'flip',title,cards});
const kase=(title,nodes)=>({t:'w',kind:'case',title,start:'a',nodes});
const chk=(q,options,why)=>({t:'check',q,options,why});
const ex=(title,prompt,steps,answer)=>({t:'example',title,prompt,steps,answer});

export const HB6={title:'Current health topics',hook:'Current health topics are 12% of HOSA Bowl—and the hardest category to cram, because the questions come from this school year’s NIH MedlinePlus Magazine editions and the CNN and NBC News health pages. The teams that do best treat current events as a year-long habit, not a last-minute search.',objectives:['Identify the official sources for current health topics.','Build a year-long system for following health news.','Extract testable facts from health articles.','Evaluate health news for accuracy and context.'],sections:[
['Official sources',[
'The references for current health topics are **NIH MedlinePlus Magazine** (current school-year editions) and the health sections of **CNN** and **NBC News**. MedlinePlus Magazine, published by the National Library of Medicine, features research from the National Institutes of Health, disease explanations, prevention tips and patient stories in plain language.',
'News health pages cover breaking stories: disease outbreaks, new drug approvals, research findings, public health guidance and health policy. Questions may ask about the main finding of a study, the agency that issued a recommendation or the disease involved in an outbreak.',
'Read the **current school year’s** editions only; older articles are less likely to be tested.',
"MedlinePlus Magazine is published several times a year, so the current school year may include only a few editions. Read each one completely, including sidebars and short features, because questions can come from any part.",
match('Source and typical content',[['MedlinePlus Magazine','NIH research and health explanations in plain language'],['CNN Health','Breaking health news and research stories'],['NBC News Health','Health news, outbreaks and policy'],['FDA announcements in news','New drug or device approvals'],['CDC guidance in news','Public health recommendations'],['WHO statements in news','Global health emergencies']])]],
['A year-long system',[
'Divide the work: assign each member one source and a weekly reading day. Each member writes a **short summary** of each important article—topic, key facts, numbers, agencies and people—in a shared document, tagged by month and topic.',
'Meet regularly to quiz each other on recent summaries. Over a year, the team builds a searchable bank of current topics instead of facing hundreds of articles at once before the conference.',
'Review older summaries regularly so early-year topics are not forgotten.',
"Use a consistent template for summaries so they are easy to scan later. A simple format—topic, date, source, key facts, numbers, related category and two questions—keeps entries short and comparable.",
order('Weekly current-topics routine',['Read assigned source','Choose the most important articles','Write short fact summaries','Add them to the shared document','Write two buzzer questions per article','Quiz teammates at the weekly meeting'])]],
['Extracting testable facts',[
'From each article, record: the **disease or condition**, the **main finding or event**, **key numbers** (percentages, case counts, ages), the **organization** involved (NIH, CDC, FDA, WHO), **names** of new drugs or technologies and **recommendations** for the public.',
'Also note the **underlying science**: what causes the condition, how it spreads and how it is treated. Questions may combine current events with basic knowledge, such as which body system a newly approved drug affects.',
'Write each fact as a buzzer-style question to practice recall.',
"Pay special attention to numbers, such as the percentage of people affected or the number of participants in a study. Numerical details are easy to test and easy to forget, so repeat them in flashcards.",
ex('Summarizing an article','Summarize a sample article (invented for practice).',['Topic: a new study on sleep and teen mental health','Finding: teens with less than 7 hours of sleep reported more anxiety','Organization: an NIH-funded research team','Advice: consistent sleep schedules and limiting late screen use','Buzzer card: “The NIH-funded study linked teens’ short sleep with higher rates of…” → anxiety'],'A compact summary captures what could be tested (article invented for practice).')]],
['Understanding health news terms',[
'Health news uses research and public health vocabulary. An **outbreak** is more cases than expected in an area; an **epidemic** is a larger increase over a region; a **pandemic** spreads across countries or continents. **Incidence** is new cases over a period; **prevalence** is all existing cases at a time.',
'Research terms include **randomized controlled trial**, **observational study**, **clinical trial phases**, **correlation versus causation** and **peer review**. FDA terms include **approval**, **emergency use authorization** and **recall**.',
'Knowing these terms helps you understand and remember news quickly.',
"Agencies use specific terms when they announce actions. The FDA “approves” or “authorizes” products and issues recalls, while the CDC issues “recommendations” and health advisories. Using the exact term helps you answer precisely.",
match('Term and meaning',[['Outbreak','More cases than expected in an area'],['Pandemic','Spread across countries or continents'],['Incidence','New cases in a time period'],['Prevalence','All existing cases at a time'],['Recall','Removing an unsafe product from the market'],['Randomized controlled trial','Participants randomly assigned to groups']])]],
['Reading news critically',[
'Headlines can exaggerate. Read beyond the headline to find **who** did the study, **how many** people were included, whether it was in **humans or animals** and whether it shows **correlation or causation**. Check whether major health agencies agree.',
'For HOSA Bowl, questions test **what the source reported**, so answer based on the article, but critical reading helps you understand and remember it.',
'Critical thinking about health news is also a lifelong skill for health professionals.',
"If two sources report the same story differently, note both versions and the date of each. Questions are written from specific references, so knowing what each source said is more useful than a general impression.",
sort('Careful reading or headline trap?',['Careful reading','Headline trap'],[['Checking if a study was in mice or people',0],['Assuming a single study proves a cure',1],['Noting the number of participants',0],['Treating correlation as causation',1],['Checking what health agencies recommend',0]])]],
['Connecting topics to other categories',[
'Current topics often link to other test categories: a new vaccine connects to **infection control** and **history of medicine**; a drug-pricing story connects to **healthcare systems**; a data-privacy breach connects to **legal and ethical practices**; a nutrition study connects to **nutrition**.',
'When summarizing, add a line about the related category and the background facts you would need. This doubles the value of each article.',
chk('A news story about a hospital data breach most directly connects to which category?',['Legal and ethical practices','Anatomy and physiology','Math conversions','Parliamentary procedure'],'Privacy laws such as HIPAA.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Current topics planning',{a:{text:'Two weeks before ILC, your team realizes no one has read MedlinePlus Magazine this year. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the best recovery?',choices:[['Divide the current editions among members, summarize key facts and quiz each other daily','b','Correct.',true],['Skip the category','b','It is 12% of the test.',false]]},b:{text:'A headline says a food “prevents cancer,” based on one observational study. How do you record it?',choices:[['Record what the article reported and note that it shows an association, not proof','c','Correct.',true],['Record it as a proven cure','c','Note the study type.',false]]},c:{text:'Next year, your team starts its weekly routine in September.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Current health topics (12%) come from current school-year NIH MedlinePlus Magazine editions and CNN and NBC News health pages.','Use a year-long system: assign sources, summarize articles weekly (topic, finding, numbers, agency, recommendations), write buzzer questions and quiz regularly.','Know health news terms (outbreak, epidemic, pandemic, incidence, prevalence, trial types, recall), read beyond headlines (correlation versus causation, human versus animal) and connect topics to other categories.'],terms:[['Incidence','The number of new cases of a condition in a period.'],['Prevalence','The total number of existing cases at a given time.'],['Pandemic','An epidemic that spreads across countries or continents.']],mcqs:[q('Which publication is an official reference for current topics?','NIH MedlinePlus Magazine','A fashion magazine','A sports almanac','An old textbook','Reference.'),q('Which news health pages are references?','CNN and NBC News','Any social media site','Celebrity blogs','None','Reference.'),q('Which editions of MedlinePlus Magazine should you study?','Current school-year editions','Editions from ten years ago','Only the first edition','None','Rule.'),q('What does prevalence measure?','All existing cases at a time','Only new cases','Deaths only','Cures','Definition.'),q('A pandemic is:','Spread across countries or continents','Limited to one town','A single case','A vaccine','Scale.'),q('Which is a headline trap?','Treating correlation as causation','Checking the study size','Reading the full article','Noting the agency','Critical reading.'),q('What is the best way to prepare for current topics?','Follow sources weekly all year','Cram the night before','Ignore them','Read only headlines','Habit.'),q('A hospital data breach story connects to:','Legal and ethical practices','Anatomy','Math','Parli pro','HIPAA.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe a year-long plan for your HOSA Bowl team to master current health topics.',rubric:[['Sources',['medlineplus magazine','current','cnn','nbc','school year']],['System',['assign','weekly','summary','shared','quiz','review']],['Facts and terms',['finding','numbers','agency','outbreak','incidence','prevalence','trial']],['Critical reading and connections',['headline','correlation','causation','study','connect','category']]],model:'Current health topics are 12% of HOSA Bowl, and the official sources are the current school year’s NIH MedlinePlus Magazine editions and the health pages of CNN and NBC News. Starting in September, we would assign one member to MedlinePlus Magazine, two members to the news sites and one member to coordinate and review, with each person reading on a set day each week. For each important article, the reader would write a short summary in our shared document tagged by month and topic: the condition, the main finding or event, key numbers, the organization involved such as NIH, CDC, FDA or WHO, any new drug or technology and public recommendations, plus a note on the background science and related test category, such as infection control for a vaccine story. Each reader would also write two buzzer-style questions per article, and we would quiz each other at weekly meetings and review older summaries monthly so early topics stay fresh. We would learn health news vocabulary—outbreak, epidemic, pandemic, incidence, prevalence, randomized controlled trial, emergency use authorization and recall—so articles are easier to understand. We would read beyond headlines to note study size, whether it involved humans or animals and whether it shows correlation or causation, while answering test items based on what the source reported. By ILC, we would have a complete, searchable bank of current topics instead of cramming.'},sources:[RULES,MAG]};

export const HB7={title:'History of medicine',hook:'From Hippocrates to penicillin, the history of medicine is a story of people who changed how we understand and treat disease. It is part of HOSA Bowl’s 40% mixed category, and its questions often ask who did what and when. Linking each pioneer to one big idea makes these facts easier to recall under pressure.',objectives:['Identify major figures in ancient and early medicine.','Describe key discoveries from the 1500s to the 1800s.','Explain twentieth-century advances in treatment and prevention.','Link pioneers to their contributions for fast recall.'],sections:[
['Ancient medicine',[
'Ancient civilizations combined observation with religious beliefs. **Egyptians** recorded treatments on papyrus and practiced embalming; **Chinese** medicine used herbs and acupuncture; **Indian** Ayurvedic medicine emphasized balance.',
'**Hippocrates** (Greek, about 460–370 BCE) is called the **father of medicine** because he taught that disease has natural causes rather than supernatural ones, emphasized observation and records and is linked to the **Hippocratic Oath**. **Galen**, a Greek physician in Rome, described anatomy (mostly from animal dissection) and influenced medicine for over a thousand years, though many of his ideas were later corrected.',
'During the **Middle Ages**, monasteries preserved medical texts, and Islamic scholars such as **Avicenna (Ibn Sina)** wrote influential medical encyclopedias.',
"Ancient medical ideas sometimes appear as context for later discoveries. The Greek theory of four “humors”—blood, phlegm, yellow bile and black bile—shaped medicine for centuries until scientific study replaced it.",
match('Pioneer and contribution',[['Hippocrates','Father of medicine; natural causes of disease'],['Galen','Anatomy texts influential for centuries'],['Avicenna','The Canon of Medicine'],['Egyptians','Early medical papyri and embalming'],['Chinese medicine','Herbal remedies and acupuncture'],['Hippocratic Oath','Ethical code for physicians']])]],
['The Renaissance and early science',[
'The Renaissance brought careful observation and experiment. **Andreas Vesalius** published **De humani corporis fabrica (1543)**, based on human dissection, correcting Galen’s errors. **William Harvey** described the **circulation of blood** pumped by the heart (1628). **Anton van Leeuwenhoek** used his microscopes to observe **microorganisms** (“animalcules”).',
'**Ambroise Paré**, a French surgeon, improved wound treatment and used ligatures to tie off blood vessels instead of cauterizing.',
'These discoveries laid the foundation for scientific medicine.',
"Microscopes were essential to these advances. Robert Hooke described “cells” after viewing cork in 1665, and Leeuwenhoek’s more powerful lenses revealed bacteria and other single-celled organisms.",
order('Discoveries in time order',['Vesalius publishes human anatomy (1543)','Harvey describes blood circulation (1628)','Leeuwenhoek observes microorganisms (1670s)','Jenner develops smallpox vaccination (1796)'])]],
['The 1800s: germs, vaccines and nursing',[
'**Edward Jenner** developed **smallpox vaccination** using cowpox (1796). **Ignaz Semmelweis** showed that **handwashing** reduced deaths from childbed fever. **Florence Nightingale** founded **modern nursing**, improving sanitation for soldiers in the Crimean War. **Louis Pasteur** supported **germ theory**, developed **pasteurization** and created vaccines for rabies and anthrax. **Joseph Lister** introduced **antiseptic surgery** using carbolic acid. **Robert Koch** identified the bacteria causing **tuberculosis** and anthrax and developed **Koch’s postulates**.',
'**Wilhelm Roentgen** discovered **X-rays** (1895). **Clara Barton** founded the **American Red Cross** (1881). **Elizabeth Blackwell** became the first woman to earn a medical degree in the United States (1849).',
"Anesthesia also transformed surgery in the 1800s. Ether was demonstrated publicly for surgery in 1846 in Boston, making longer and more complex operations possible and, combined with antisepsis, much safer.",
"Women pioneers are frequently tested: besides Nightingale, Barton and Blackwell, Rosalind Franklin’s DNA images and Dorothea Dix’s advocacy for people with mental illness are notable contributions.",
flip('1800s pioneers',[['Semmelweis','Handwashing reduces deaths'],['Nightingale','Founder of modern nursing'],['Pasteur','Germ theory and pasteurization'],['Lister','Antiseptic surgery'],['Koch','Tuberculosis bacterium; postulates'],['Roentgen','X-rays (1895)']])]],
['The twentieth century',[
'**Alexander Fleming** discovered **penicillin** (1928), the first widely used antibiotic. **Frederick Banting and Charles Best** discovered **insulin** (1921), transforming diabetes care. **Jonas Salk** developed the **injected polio vaccine** (1955), and **Albert Sabin** developed the **oral** polio vaccine.',
'**James Watson and Francis Crick**, using **Rosalind Franklin’s** X-ray images, described the **double-helix structure of DNA** (1953). **Christiaan Barnard** performed the first human **heart transplant** (1967). Smallpox was declared **eradicated** in 1980. Imaging advanced with CT and MRI.',
'Twentieth-century advances dramatically increased life expectancy.',
"Other notable twentieth-century firsts include the development of the electrocardiogram by Willem Einthoven, early kidney dialysis and the first successful kidney transplant in 1954 between identical twins.",
match('Advance and person',[['Penicillin','Alexander Fleming'],['Insulin','Banting and Best'],['Injected polio vaccine','Jonas Salk'],['Oral polio vaccine','Albert Sabin'],['DNA double helix','Watson, Crick and Franklin'],['First human heart transplant','Christiaan Barnard']])]],
['Modern milestones',[
'Recent decades brought the **Human Genome Project** (completed in 2003), **minimally invasive and robotic surgery**, **organ transplantation** advances, **antiretroviral therapy** for HIV, **targeted cancer therapies** and **mRNA vaccines**. Electronic health records and telehealth changed how care is delivered.',
'Modern history often appears in current health topics, so connect new advances to their historical roots—mRNA vaccines build on Jenner’s and Pasteur’s work on immunity.',
'DHO: Health Science includes a history chapter; review its timeline.',
"Public health milestones are part of history too: clean water and sanitation, widespread vaccination and tobacco control have saved millions of lives and are often credited with large gains in life expectancy.",
chk('Which project mapped the human genome?',['The Human Genome Project','The Manhattan Project','The Apollo Program','The Framingham Study'],'Completed in 2003.')]],
['Remembering history for the buzzer',[
'Link each pioneer to **one keyword**: Fleming–penicillin, Lister–antiseptic, Jenner–smallpox, Harvey–circulation, Vesalius–anatomy, Nightingale–nursing. Build a **timeline** to see how discoveries built on each other.',
'Watch for look-alikes: **Salk** (injected) versus **Sabin** (oral) polio vaccines; **Pasteur** (germ theory, pasteurization) versus **Lister** (applied germ theory to surgery) versus **Koch** (identified specific bacteria).',
"Group pioneers by theme as well as by date—vaccines (Jenner, Pasteur, Salk, Sabin), surgery (Paré, Lister, Barnard), infection (Semmelweis, Nightingale, Koch) and imaging (Roentgen). Theme groups make it easier to answer “which of the following” items.",
ex('Pairing look-alikes','Distinguish three germ-theory pioneers.',['Pasteur: proved microbes cause fermentation and disease; pasteurization; rabies vaccine','Lister: used carbolic acid antisepsis in surgery','Koch: identified specific bacteria such as the tuberculosis bacterium; Koch’s postulates'],'Pasteur = theory, Lister = surgery, Koch = specific bacteria.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('History drill',{a:{text:'“The physician known as the father of medicine is…” (Practice item.)',choices:[['Hippocrates','b','Correct.',true],['Galen','b','Galen was influential, but Hippocrates is the father of medicine.',false]]},b:{text:'“The scientist who discovered penicillin in 1928 was…”',choices:[['Alexander Fleming','c','Correct.',true],['Louis Pasteur','c','Fleming discovered penicillin.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team adds both to its pioneer keyword deck.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Ancient: Hippocrates (father of medicine; natural causes; Oath), Galen (anatomy texts), Avicenna (Canon); Renaissance: Vesalius (1543 anatomy), Harvey (1628 circulation), Leeuwenhoek (microorganisms), Paré (ligatures).','1800s: Jenner (smallpox vaccine, 1796), Semmelweis (handwashing), Nightingale (modern nursing), Pasteur (germ theory, pasteurization), Lister (antisepsis), Koch (TB bacterium), Roentgen (X-rays, 1895), Barton (Red Cross), Blackwell (first U.S. woman MD).','1900s and beyond: Fleming (penicillin, 1928), Banting and Best (insulin), Salk (injected) and Sabin (oral) polio vaccines, DNA double helix (1953), Barnard (heart transplant, 1967), smallpox eradicated (1980), Human Genome Project (2003); learn keywords and timelines.'],terms:[['Germ theory','The idea that microorganisms cause many diseases.'],['Antisepsis','Preventing infection by killing microbes on tissues and surfaces.'],['Vaccination','Stimulating immunity to protect against a disease.']],mcqs:[q('Who is known as the father of medicine?','Hippocrates','Galen','Pasteur','Lister','Ancient Greece.'),q('Who described the circulation of blood?','William Harvey','Andreas Vesalius','Robert Koch','Edward Jenner','1628.'),q('Who developed smallpox vaccination?','Edward Jenner','Jonas Salk','Joseph Lister','Alexander Fleming','1796.'),q('Who introduced antiseptic surgery?','Joseph Lister','Louis Pasteur','Florence Nightingale','William Harvey','Carbolic acid.'),q('Who discovered penicillin?','Alexander Fleming','Robert Koch','Albert Sabin','Christiaan Barnard','1928.'),q('Who founded modern nursing?','Florence Nightingale','Clara Barton','Elizabeth Blackwell','Rosalind Franklin','Crimean War.'),q('Who discovered X-rays?','Wilhelm Roentgen','Louis Pasteur','Edward Jenner','Galen','1895.'),q('Who developed the oral polio vaccine?','Albert Sabin','Jonas Salk','Joseph Lister','Robert Koch','Oral.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe key figures in the history of medicine from ancient times to the twentieth century and explain how your team would remember them.',rubric:[['Ancient and Renaissance',['hippocrates','galen','vesalius','harvey','leeuwenhoek']],['1800s',['jenner','semmelweis','nightingale','pasteur','lister','koch','roentgen']],['1900s',['fleming','penicillin','insulin','salk','sabin','dna','barnard']],['Study method',['keyword','timeline','look-alikes','cards','buzzer']]],model:'Hippocrates, the ancient Greek known as the father of medicine, taught that disease has natural causes and emphasized observation; Galen’s anatomy texts influenced medicine for centuries until Andreas Vesalius corrected many errors with human dissection in his 1543 book. William Harvey described the circulation of blood in 1628, and Anton van Leeuwenhoek first observed microorganisms with his microscopes. In 1796, Edward Jenner developed smallpox vaccination. In the 1800s, Ignaz Semmelweis showed that handwashing saved lives, Florence Nightingale founded modern nursing, Louis Pasteur supported germ theory and developed pasteurization and rabies vaccine, Joseph Lister introduced antiseptic surgery with carbolic acid, Robert Koch identified the tuberculosis bacterium and Wilhelm Roentgen discovered X-rays in 1895, while Clara Barton founded the American Red Cross. In the twentieth century, Alexander Fleming discovered penicillin in 1928, Banting and Best discovered insulin, Jonas Salk developed the injected polio vaccine and Albert Sabin the oral vaccine, Watson and Crick, using Rosalind Franklin’s images, described DNA’s double helix in 1953 and Christiaan Barnard performed the first human heart transplant in 1967. To remember these facts, we would link each person to one keyword, build a timeline showing how discoveries built on one another, drill look-alikes such as Salk versus Sabin and Pasteur versus Lister versus Koch and practice with buzzer cards.'},sources:[RULES,NLM]};

export const HB8={title:'Healthcare systems, careers, law and ethics',hook:'Who pays for care? What is the difference between Medicare and Medicaid? Which professional may perform which tasks? What does HIPAA protect? Healthcare systems, health careers and legal and ethical practices all fall within HOSA Bowl’s 40% mixed category, and they share one theme: how health care is organized and delivered responsibly.',objectives:['Describe types of healthcare facilities and payment systems.','Distinguish major health insurance programs and plan types.','Identify health career clusters and credentialing terms.','Apply key legal and ethical principles in health care.'],sections:[
['Healthcare facilities and agencies',[
'Health care is delivered in **hospitals** (general, specialty, government), **long-term care facilities** (nursing homes, assisted living), **medical and dental offices**, **clinics**, **urgent care centers**, **home health agencies**, **hospice**, **rehabilitation centers** and **laboratories**. **Public health agencies** include the **CDC** (disease prevention), **FDA** (food and drug safety), **NIH** (research), **OSHA** (workplace safety) and the **WHO** (international health).',
'Voluntary and nonprofit agencies such as the American Heart Association and American Cancer Society fund research and education.',
'Know what each agency does—buzzer items often ask which agency is responsible for a function.',
"Health care also includes government facilities such as Veterans Affairs hospitals, military health care and public health departments that provide immunizations, screenings and disease surveillance in local communities.",
match('Agency and role',[['CDC','Disease prevention and control'],['FDA','Food, drug and device safety'],['NIH','Medical research'],['OSHA','Workplace safety'],['WHO','International health'],['Hospice','Comfort care at the end of life']])]],
['Paying for health care',[
'**Private health insurance** is often provided through employers. Key terms include **premium** (regular payment for coverage), **deductible** (amount paid before insurance pays), **copayment** (fixed fee per visit) and **coinsurance** (percentage of costs shared). **Health maintenance organizations (HMOs)** usually require a primary care provider and referrals; **preferred provider organizations (PPOs)** allow more choice at higher cost.',
'**Medicare** is a federal program mainly for people **65 and older** and some people with disabilities; it has **Parts A (hospital), B (medical), C (Medicare Advantage) and D (prescription drugs)**. **Medicaid** is a joint **federal-state** program for people with **limited income**. Workers’ compensation covers work-related injuries.',
'Medicare = mainly age; Medicaid = mainly income.',
"The Affordable Care Act of 2010 expanded access to insurance through marketplaces and Medicaid expansion in participating states. Questions about current health policy often connect back to these programs.",
"Insurance may also be described as fee-for-service, in which each service is billed separately, or as managed care, in which networks and fixed payments help control costs.",
sort('Medicare or Medicaid?',['Medicare','Medicaid'],[['Mainly for people 65 and older',0],['Joint federal and state program',1],['Based mainly on limited income',1],['Has Parts A, B, C and D',0],['Covers some younger people with disabilities',0]])]],
['Health careers and credentials',[
'Health careers are often grouped into pathways: **therapeutic services**, **diagnostic services**, **health informatics**, **support services** and **biotechnology research and development**. Careers range from entry-level assistants to professionals with doctoral degrees.',
'**Licensure** is granted by a state and is legally required for certain professions; **certification** is usually granted by a professional organization after an exam; **registration** lists qualified professionals. **Continuing education units (CEUs)** keep credentials current. **Scope of practice** defines what a professional is legally allowed to do.',
'Know the education level and main duties of common careers.',
"Health care teams also include supervisors and team leaders. Understanding the chain of command and when to report concerns to a supervisor is both a career skill and a legal responsibility.",
"Professional associations, such as those for nurses, therapists or technologists, set practice standards and offer certifications. Knowing the major credentialing organization for a few common careers can help with career questions.",
match('Career and pathway',[['Registered nurse','Therapeutic services'],['Radiologic technologist','Diagnostic services'],['Medical coder','Health informatics'],['Central sterile technician','Support services'],['Biomedical engineer','Biotechnology research and development'],['Pharmacist','Therapeutic services']])]],
['Legal responsibilities',[
'**Negligence** is failure to provide expected care, causing harm; **malpractice** is negligence by a professional. **Assault** is a threat or attempt to touch without consent; **battery** is unauthorized touching. **False imprisonment** is restraining someone without authority. **Invasion of privacy** includes sharing private information. **Defamation** is false statements damaging a reputation (**slander** spoken, **libel** written).',
'**Informed consent** means the patient understands the procedure, risks and alternatives and agrees. **HIPAA** protects the privacy of health information. **Advance directives**, such as a living will and durable power of attorney for health care, state patients’ wishes.',
'Most legal questions ask you to match a situation with a term.',
"Health professionals are also mandated reporters in many situations, such as suspected child abuse or certain communicable diseases. Failing to report when required can have legal consequences.",
sort('Which legal term?',['Battery','Negligence','Invasion of privacy'],[['Performing a procedure without consent',0],['Forgetting to raise bed rails, leading to a fall',1],['Sharing a patient’s diagnosis with a friend',2],['Giving an injection after the patient refused',0],['Failing to report a change in condition',1],['Posting a patient photo online',2]])]],
['Ethics in health care',[
'**Ethics** are moral principles of right and wrong. Key principles include **autonomy** (respecting patients’ decisions), **beneficence** (doing good), **nonmaleficence** (doing no harm), **justice** (fairness) and **confidentiality**. Professionals follow codes of ethics and avoid accepting gifts that create conflicts, performing tasks outside their training or discriminating against patients.',
'The **Patient’s Bill of Rights** describes rights such as respectful care, information, privacy and refusing treatment.',
'Ethical dilemmas often involve conflicts between principles, such as autonomy and beneficence.',
"Ethical behavior also includes honesty about mistakes. Reporting an error immediately protects the patient and allows it to be corrected, even when admitting it is uncomfortable.",
match('Principle and example',[['Autonomy','Respecting a patient’s refusal of treatment'],['Beneficence','Acting in the patient’s best interest'],['Nonmaleficence','Avoiding harm'],['Justice','Fair distribution of care'],['Confidentiality','Keeping health information private'],['Informed consent','Explaining risks before a procedure']])]],
['Linking these topics',[
'These categories connect: insurance affects **access** (justice); scope of practice is both a **career** and a **legal** topic; HIPAA is both **legal** and **ethical**; Medicare is a **healthcare system** topic that often appears in **current health news**.',
'Study them together using DHO: Health Science chapters on healthcare systems, careers and legal and ethical responsibilities.',
"When studying, make a two-column chart of legal and ethical terms with a short example for each. Examples help you recognize the term when a buzzer item describes a situation rather than giving a definition.",
chk('A medical assistant performs a task their state does not allow. Which concept is violated?',['Scope of practice','Deductible','Premium','Pasteurization'],'Legal limits on duties.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Systems and ethics drill',{a:{text:'“A joint federal-state program that provides health coverage for people with limited income is…” (Practice item.)',choices:[['Medicaid','b','Correct.',true],['Medicare','b','Medicare is mainly for people 65 and older.',false]]},b:{text:'“Touching a patient without consent is called…”',choices:[['Battery','c','Correct.',true],['Assault','c','Assault is the threat or attempt; battery is the touching.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team adds both to its “tricky pairs” list.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Know facilities (hospitals, long-term care, home health, hospice) and agencies (CDC, FDA, NIH, OSHA, WHO); insurance terms (premium, deductible, copayment, coinsurance, HMO, PPO); Medicare (mainly 65+, Parts A–D) versus Medicaid (federal-state, limited income).','Health careers fall into therapeutic, diagnostic, informatics, support and biotechnology pathways; licensure (state), certification (organization), registration, CEUs and scope of practice.','Legal terms: negligence, malpractice, assault, battery, false imprisonment, invasion of privacy, defamation, informed consent, HIPAA, advance directives; ethics: autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence, justice, confidentiality and the Patient’s Bill of Rights.'],terms:[['Deductible','The amount paid before insurance begins to pay.'],['Scope of practice','The tasks a professional is legally allowed to perform.'],['Battery','Touching a person without consent.']],mcqs:[q('Medicare mainly covers people who are:','65 and older','Under 18','Low-income only','Veterans only','Age.'),q('Medicaid is:','A joint federal-state program for limited income','Only for people 65+','A private insurer','A hospital','Income.'),q('Which agency ensures drug safety?','FDA','OSHA','CDC','WHO','Drugs.'),q('A deductible is:','The amount paid before insurance pays','A monthly fee','A percentage after each visit','A gift','Insurance.'),q('Which plan usually requires referrals?','HMO','PPO','Medicare Part D','Workers’ compensation','Gatekeeper.'),q('Touching a patient without consent is:','Battery','Slander','Libel','Justice','Legal term.'),q('Which principle means “do no harm”?','Nonmaleficence','Autonomy','Justice','Beneficence','Ethics.'),q('Licensure is granted by:','A state','A professional organization only','A patient','An insurance company','Credential.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain key concepts in healthcare systems, health careers and legal and ethical practice that a HOSA Bowl team should know.',rubric:[['Systems and agencies',['hospital','long-term','cdc','fda','nih','osha','who']],['Payment',['premium','deductible','copayment','hmo','ppo','medicare','medicaid']],['Careers',['pathways','licensure','certification','scope of practice','ceu']],['Law and ethics',['negligence','malpractice','battery','hipaa','informed consent','autonomy','nonmaleficence']]],model:'Health care is delivered in hospitals, long-term care facilities, offices, clinics, urgent care centers, home health agencies, hospice and rehabilitation centers, supported by agencies such as the CDC for disease prevention, the FDA for food and drug safety, the NIH for research, OSHA for workplace safety and the WHO for international health. Most Americans pay through private insurance, where the premium is the regular payment, the deductible is paid before insurance begins, a copayment is a fixed fee and coinsurance is a shared percentage; HMOs usually require a primary care provider and referrals, while PPOs offer more choice. Medicare is a federal program mainly for people 65 and older, with Parts A, B, C and D, while Medicaid is a joint federal-state program for people with limited income. Health careers fall into therapeutic, diagnostic, health informatics, support and biotechnology research and development pathways; licensure is granted by a state, certification by a professional organization and scope of practice sets legal limits on duties. Legal concepts include negligence and malpractice, assault as a threat and battery as unauthorized touching, false imprisonment, invasion of privacy and defamation, as well as informed consent, HIPAA privacy protections and advance directives. Ethical principles include autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence, justice and confidentiality, along with the Patient’s Bill of Rights. We would study these topics together from DHO: Health Science and drill tricky pairs such as Medicare versus Medicaid and assault versus battery.'},sources:[RULES,MEDICARE,HIPAA]};

export const HB9={title:'Nutrition, safety and infection control',hook:'Nutrition, safety and infection control are everyday knowledge for health professionals—and three more parts of HOSA Bowl’s 40% mixed category. Questions might ask how many calories a gram of fat provides, what RACE stands for or which link of the chain of infection handwashing breaks. These are concrete facts that reward careful memorization.',objectives:['Recall essential nutrients, their functions and calorie values.','Describe body mechanics and fire and chemical safety.','Explain the chain of infection and how to break it.','Distinguish standard and transmission-based precautions and asepsis.'],sections:[
['Essential nutrients',[
'The six classes of nutrients are **carbohydrates**, **proteins**, **fats (lipids)**, **vitamins**, **minerals** and **water**. Carbohydrates are the main energy source; proteins build and repair tissue; fats store energy, insulate and help absorb fat-soluble vitamins. **Carbohydrates and proteins provide 4 calories per gram; fats provide 9.**',
'**Fat-soluble vitamins** are **A, D, E and K**; water-soluble vitamins include **C** and the **B vitamins**. Key minerals include **calcium** (bones), **iron** (hemoglobin) and **sodium and potassium** (fluid balance and nerve function). Water is the most essential nutrient.',
'The **Dietary Guidelines for Americans** and **MyPlate** give recommendations for healthy eating patterns.',
"Minerals are divided into major minerals, needed in larger amounts (such as calcium, phosphorus, magnesium, sodium and potassium), and trace minerals, needed in small amounts (such as iron, zinc and iodine). Iodine deficiency can cause goiter.",
match('Nutrient and function or fact',[['Carbohydrates','Main energy source; 4 calories per gram'],['Protein','Builds and repairs tissue; 4 calories per gram'],['Fat','Stores energy; 9 calories per gram'],['Vitamin D','Fat-soluble; helps absorb calcium'],['Iron','Needed for hemoglobin'],['Water','Most essential nutrient']])]],
['Therapeutic diets and nutrition problems',[
'Therapeutic diets modify a regular diet: **clear liquid** (broth, gelatin, clear juices), **full liquid**, **soft**, **diabetic** (controlled carbohydrates), **sodium-restricted** (for heart and kidney disease), **low-fat or low-cholesterol**, **high-protein** and **bland** diets.',
'Nutrition problems include **obesity**, **malnutrition**, **eating disorders** (anorexia nervosa, bulimia nervosa, binge-eating disorder), **vitamin deficiencies** (scurvy from lack of vitamin C; rickets from lack of vitamin D) and **dehydration**.',
'Pair each deficiency with its vitamin for quick recall.',
"Know how diets progress after surgery or illness: patients often move from clear liquids to full liquids, then to soft foods and finally to a regular diet as tolerated, following the provider’s orders.",
sort('Diet type for the condition?',['Sodium-restricted','Diabetic','Clear liquid'],[['Hypertension',0],['Type 2 diabetes',1],['Just after some surgeries',2],['Heart failure',0],['Carbohydrate control',1],['Broth and gelatin',2]])]],
['Workplace and body mechanics safety',[
'**Body mechanics** prevent injury: keep a **broad base of support** with feet apart, **bend at the hips and knees** rather than the waist, keep loads **close to the body**, **avoid twisting**, push or pull rather than lift when possible and get help for heavy loads.',
'**Patient safety** includes identifying the patient, using bed rails and call lights as ordered, locking wheelchair and bed wheels, reporting hazards and following facility policies. **Safety Data Sheets (SDS)** describe chemical hazards and handling, and **OSHA** sets workplace safety standards.',
'Accidents and errors must be reported promptly, usually with an incident report.',
"Ergonomics is the science of fitting the workplace to the worker, such as adjusting computer stations and using mechanical lifts. OSHA provides guidance on ergonomics and safe patient handling to reduce injuries.",
sort('Correct body mechanics?',['Correct','Incorrect'],[['Bending knees and hips to lift',0],['Twisting while lifting',1],['Keeping the load close to the body',0],['Lifting with a narrow stance',1],['Asking for help with a heavy patient',0]])]],
['Fire and emergency safety',[
'Fires need **heat, fuel and oxygen** (the fire triangle); removing any one stops the fire. Health facilities use **RACE**: **Rescue** people in danger, **Alarm** (activate the alarm), **Contain** (close doors and windows) and **Extinguish or Evacuate**. To use an extinguisher, remember **PASS**: **Pull** the pin, **Aim** at the base of the fire, **Squeeze** the handle and **Sweep** side to side.',
'Extinguisher classes include **Class A** (ordinary combustibles), **Class B** (flammable liquids), **Class C** (electrical) and **Class ABC** (multipurpose). Oxygen use increases fire risk, so no open flames are allowed near it.',
"Facilities also use emergency codes for situations such as fires, cardiac arrest or missing patients. Codes vary between facilities, so health workers learn the codes used where they work.",
order('RACE in a fire',['Rescue','Alarm','Contain','Extinguish or evacuate'])]],
['The chain of infection',[
'Infection spreads through six links: an **infectious agent** (bacteria, viruses, fungi, protozoa), a **reservoir** (where it lives), a **portal of exit**, a **mode of transmission** (contact, droplet, airborne, vehicle or vector), a **portal of entry** and a **susceptible host**. Breaking any link stops the spread.',
'**Hand hygiene** is the single most important way to prevent infection, breaking the transmission link. Other measures include cleaning and disinfection, PPE, vaccination (protecting the host) and proper waste disposal.',
'Microorganisms that cause disease are **pathogens**; **nosocomial** or **healthcare-associated infections** are acquired in health care settings.',
"Different microorganisms require different control measures. Bacterial spores, for example, resist many disinfectants, which is why sterilization is required for instruments that enter sterile body tissues.",
order('Chain of infection',['Infectious agent','Reservoir','Portal of exit','Mode of transmission','Portal of entry','Susceptible host'])]],
['Precautions and asepsis',[
'**Standard precautions** apply to all patients: hand hygiene, PPE when contact with blood or body fluids is possible, safe sharps handling, respiratory hygiene and cleaning. **Transmission-based precautions** are added for known or suspected infections: **contact** (gown and gloves), **droplet** (mask) and **airborne** (N95 respirator and an airborne infection isolation room, for diseases such as tuberculosis).',
'**Asepsis** means being free of pathogens. **Antisepsis** inhibits microorganisms on skin; **disinfection** kills most pathogens on surfaces; **sterilization** destroys **all** microorganisms, including spores, often with an **autoclave** (steam under pressure).',
'OSHA’s Bloodborne Pathogens Standard protects workers from HIV, hepatitis B and hepatitis C.',
"Proper PPE sequence matters: put on (don) gown, mask or respirator, goggles or face shield, then gloves; remove (doff) in an order that avoids contaminating yourself, typically starting with gloves, and perform hand hygiene afterward.",
match('Precaution or process and example',[['Contact precautions','Gown and gloves'],['Droplet precautions','Mask'],['Airborne precautions','N95 respirator and negative-pressure room'],['Sterilization','Destroys all microorganisms, including spores'],['Disinfection','Kills most pathogens on surfaces'],['Autoclave','Steam under pressure']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Mixed-category drill',{a:{text:'“How many calories does one gram of fat provide?” (Practice item.)',choices:[['9','b','Correct.',true],['4','b','Carbohydrates and proteins provide 4; fat provides 9.',false]]},b:{text:'“In the PASS technique, the A stands for…”',choices:[['Aim at the base of the fire','c','Correct.',true],['Alarm','c','Alarm is part of RACE.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team adds a RACE-versus-PASS card to its deck.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Six nutrient classes; carbohydrates and proteins 4 kcal/g, fat 9 kcal/g; fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, K; therapeutic diets (clear liquid, diabetic, sodium-restricted); deficiencies such as scurvy (C) and rickets (D).','Safety: body mechanics (broad base, bend knees and hips, load close, no twisting), SDS, incident reports; fire triangle; RACE (Rescue, Alarm, Contain, Extinguish/Evacuate); PASS (Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep); extinguisher classes A, B, C, ABC.','Chain of infection: agent, reservoir, portal of exit, transmission, portal of entry, susceptible host; hand hygiene is most important; standard versus contact, droplet and airborne precautions; antisepsis, disinfection and sterilization (autoclave).'],terms:[['Chain of infection','The six links required for infection to spread.'],['Sterilization','Destruction of all microorganisms, including spores.'],['Body mechanics','Using the body efficiently and safely to prevent injury.']],mcqs:[q('How many calories per gram does protein provide?','4','9','7','2','Macronutrients.'),q('Which vitamins are fat-soluble?','A, D, E and K','B and C','C only','All vitamins','Fat-soluble.'),q('What does the R in RACE stand for?','Rescue','Run','Report','Remove','Fire safety.'),q('In PASS, the first step is to:','Pull the pin','Sweep','Squeeze','Aim','Extinguisher.'),q('Which is the most important way to prevent infection?','Hand hygiene','Antibiotics for everyone','Closing windows','Wearing jewelry','Breaks transmission.'),q('Which precaution requires an N95 respirator?','Airborne','Contact','Droplet','Standard only','Tuberculosis.'),q('What destroys all microorganisms, including spores?','Sterilization','Antisepsis','Disinfection','Sanitizing','Complete.'),q('Which is correct body mechanics?','Bend at the hips and knees','Twist while lifting','Keep feet together','Hold loads far away','Safety.')],frq:{prompt:'Summarize the nutrition, safety and infection control facts your HOSA Bowl team should master.',rubric:[['Nutrition',['carbohydrates','protein','fat','4','9','a, d, e','k','diet']],['Safety',['body mechanics','knees','sds','race','pass','fire triangle']],['Chain of infection',['agent','reservoir','exit','transmission','entry','host','hand hygiene']],['Precautions',['standard','contact','droplet','airborne','n95','sterilization','autoclave']]],model:'For nutrition, we would learn the six nutrient classes—carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, minerals and water—and that carbohydrates and proteins provide 4 calories per gram while fat provides 9; that vitamins A, D, E and K are fat-soluble and vitamin C and the B vitamins are water-soluble; that calcium supports bones and iron is needed for hemoglobin; and that therapeutic diets include clear liquid, soft, diabetic and sodium-restricted diets, with deficiencies such as scurvy from lack of vitamin C and rickets from lack of vitamin D. For safety, we would know good body mechanics—a broad base of support, bending at the hips and knees, keeping loads close and avoiding twisting—along with Safety Data Sheets for chemicals and incident reports. For fire safety, we would memorize the fire triangle of heat, fuel and oxygen, RACE for Rescue, Alarm, Contain and Extinguish or Evacuate, PASS for Pull, Aim, Squeeze and Sweep, and extinguisher classes A, B, C and ABC. For infection control, we would know the six links of the chain of infection—infectious agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission, portal of entry and susceptible host—and that hand hygiene is the most important way to break it. We would distinguish standard precautions for all patients from contact (gown and gloves), droplet (mask) and airborne (N95 respirator) precautions, and antisepsis, disinfection and sterilization, which destroys all microorganisms, often with an autoclave.'},sources:[RULES,DGA,ISO]};

export const HB10={title:'Math conversions and match-day strategy',hook:'Math conversions are the final piece of HOSA Bowl’s 40% mixed category—and a place where quick mental math wins points. Converting pounds to kilograms, Fahrenheit to Celsius or standard time to military time in five seconds takes practice. This lesson finishes with test-day and match-day strategies to bring all of your preparation together.',objectives:['Convert common health care units quickly and accurately.','Convert temperatures and 24-hour clock times.','Use test-taking strategies for the 50-item Round One test.','Apply match-day routines for buzzer rounds.'],sections:[
['Metric basics',[
'The metric system uses base units—**meter** (length), **gram** (mass) and **liter** (volume)—with prefixes: **kilo-** (1,000), **centi-** (1/100), **milli-** (1/1,000) and **micro-** (1/1,000,000). To convert, move the decimal point: 1 kg = 1,000 g; 1 g = 1,000 mg; 1 mg = 1,000 mcg; 1 L = 1,000 mL; 1 m = 100 cm = 1,000 mm.',
'A **cubic centimeter (cc)** equals **1 mL**. Moving from a larger unit to a smaller unit means multiplying (more units); moving to a larger unit means dividing.',
'Practice decimal shifts until they are automatic.',
"Avoid trailing zeros and always use leading zeros when writing doses: write 1 mg, not 1.0 mg, and 0.5 mg, not .5 mg. These rules prevent tenfold medication errors and sometimes appear in safety questions.",
match('Metric equivalent',[['1 kilogram','1,000 grams'],['1 gram','1,000 milligrams'],['1 milligram','1,000 micrograms'],['1 liter','1,000 milliliters'],['1 cc','1 mL'],['1 meter','100 centimeters']])]],
['Household and metric conversions',[
'Common health care equivalents: **1 kg = 2.2 lb**; **1 inch = 2.54 cm**; **1 teaspoon (tsp) = 5 mL**; **1 tablespoon (Tbsp) = 15 mL** (3 tsp); **1 fluid ounce = 30 mL**; **1 cup = 8 fl oz = 240 mL**; **1 pint = 2 cups**; **1 quart = 2 pints**; **1 gallon = 4 quarts**.',
'To convert pounds to kilograms, **divide by 2.2**; kilograms to pounds, **multiply by 2.2**. To convert inches to centimeters, **multiply by 2.54**.',
"Some conversions combine steps. To find a child’s dose based on weight, for example, you may first convert pounds to kilograms and then multiply by a dose per kilogram. Practice multi-step problems so each step becomes quick.",
ex('Weight conversion','A patient weighs 154 lb. What is the weight in kilograms?',['Use 1 kg = 2.2 lb','154 ÷ 2.2 = 70','Check: 70 × 2.2 = 154'],'70 kg.')]],
['Temperature conversions',[
'Convert Fahrenheit to Celsius with **°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9**, and Celsius to Fahrenheit with **°F = (°C × 9/5) + 32**. Useful anchors: water freezes at **32°F = 0°C** and boils at **212°F = 100°C**; normal body temperature is about **98.6°F = 37°C**.',
'For speed, memorize a few common fever values: **100.4°F = 38°C**, **102.2°F = 39°C**, **104°F = 40°C**.',
ex('Temperature conversion','Convert 101.3°F to Celsius.',['Subtract 32: 101.3 − 32 = 69.3','Multiply by 5/9: 69.3 × 5 = 346.5; 346.5 ÷ 9 = 38.5','Answer to one decimal place'],'38.5°C.')]],
['The 24-hour clock',[
'Health care uses the **24-hour clock** (military time) to avoid confusion between a.m. and p.m. Midnight is **0000** (or 2400 at the end of a day), noon is **1200** and times after noon add **12**: 1:00 p.m. = **1300**, 6:30 p.m. = **1830**, 11:45 p.m. = **2345**. For morning times, add a leading zero: 7:15 a.m. = **0715**.',
'To convert back, subtract 12 from times greater than 1259 and add p.m.: 1645 = 4:45 p.m.',
'Read times aloud as “fourteen hundred” or “zero seven fifteen.”',
sort('Correct 24-hour time?',['Correct','Incorrect'],[['3:00 p.m. = 1500',0],['7:30 a.m. = 0730',0],['9:00 p.m. = 0900',1],['Noon = 1200',0],['12:30 a.m. = 1230',1],['5:45 p.m. = 1745',0]])]],
['Round One test strategy',[
'The test has **50 multiple-choice items in 60 minutes**, about **72 seconds per item**, with **no verbal time announcements**—each member must watch the clock. The team’s **average** score determines qualification and seeding, so every member’s score matters.',
'Strategies: answer easy items first and mark hard ones to revisit; read every option before choosing; watch for **“except,” “not” and “all of the following”**; eliminate clearly wrong choices; and never leave an item blank if there is no penalty for guessing. Bring two #2 pencils with erasers and, optionally, neon foam earplugs.',
'Since seeding affects your bracket, a strong test score earns an easier path.',
"Work on the test individually but prepare together. Because the average counts, teams benefit most when every member studies the full test plan rather than leaving categories to one expert.",
ex('Pacing the test','Plan pacing for 50 items in 60 minutes.',['60 minutes ÷ 50 items = 1.2 minutes (72 seconds) per item','Checkpoint: about item 25 at 30 minutes','Leave about 5 minutes to review marked items','Answer every item before time ends'],'About 72 seconds per item, with checkpoints and review time.')]],
['Match-day routine',[
'Before each match, **test your buzzer**, sit comfortably and take a calm breath. Remember your **letter** for the round and listen carefully—during the first 8 minutes, only your matching letter may buzz. Keep **silent** until the final 2 minutes, then communicate softly and quickly.',
'After a miss, reset immediately. Between matches, stay in the holding room, avoid talking with other teams, review a few flashcards and keep energy positive. Dress professionally—teams not properly dressed may not compete.',
'Trust your preparation; confident, accurate answers win more points than rushed ones.',
"Review your team’s most-missed flashcards the morning of each match day rather than trying to learn new material. Familiar content recalled quickly is worth more than new facts learned under pressure.",
order('Before each buzzer match',['Check professional dress','Test your buzzer','Note your letter for the round','Take a calm breath','Listen to the full item when unsure','Answer clearly and completely'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Conversion and match drill',{a:{text:'“How many milliliters are in 2 teaspoons?” (Practice item.)',choices:[['10 mL','b','Correct—1 tsp = 5 mL.',true],['30 mL','b','30 mL is 1 fluid ounce.',false]]},b:{text:'“What is 2:20 p.m. in 24-hour time?”',choices:[['1420','c','Correct.',true],['0220','c','Add 12 to afternoon hours.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team finishes its final mock round confident and ready.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Metric prefixes: kilo (1,000), centi (1/100), milli (1/1,000), micro (1/1,000,000); 1 cc = 1 mL; household equivalents: 1 kg = 2.2 lb, 1 in = 2.54 cm, 1 tsp = 5 mL, 1 Tbsp = 15 mL, 1 fl oz = 30 mL, 1 cup = 240 mL.','°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9 and °F = (°C × 9/5) + 32; 98.6°F = 37°C; 24-hour clock adds 12 to p.m. hours (1:00 p.m. = 1300; midnight = 0000).','Test: 50 items in 60 minutes (about 72 seconds each), no time announcements, team average seeds the bracket; match day: test buzzers, know your letter, stay silent for 8 minutes, reset after misses and stay professional.'],terms:[['Metric prefix','A word part showing a multiple or fraction of a base unit.'],['24-hour clock','A time system from 0000 to 2359 used in health care.'],['Conversion factor','A ratio used to change units, such as 1 kg = 2.2 lb.']],mcqs:[q('How many pounds are in 1 kilogram?','2.2','1','10','0.45','Conversion.'),q('How many milliliters are in 1 teaspoon?','5','15','30','1','Household.'),q('What is 98.6°F in Celsius?','37°C','40°C','32°C','100°C','Body temperature.'),q('What is 6:30 p.m. in 24-hour time?','1830','0630','1630','2030','Add 12.'),q('How many milligrams are in 1 gram?','1,000','100','10','1,000,000','Metric.'),q('A patient weighs 88 kg. About how many pounds is that?','194 lb','40 lb','88 lb','176 lb','88 × 2.2 = 193.6.'),q('How many mL are in 1 fluid ounce?','30','5','15','240','Household.'),q('About how long is available per item on the Round One test?','About 72 seconds','About 10 seconds','About 5 minutes','About 30 seconds','60 minutes ÷ 50.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the math conversions HOSA Bowl competitors should master and describe test-day and match-day strategies.',rubric:[['Metric and household',['kilo','milli','micro','1,000','2.2','2.54','5 ml','30 ml']],['Temperature and time',['°c','°f','32','5/9','37','24-hour','1300','add 12']],['Test strategy',['50','60 minutes','72 seconds','except','eliminate','average','seed']],['Match routine',['buzzer','letter','silent','8 minutes','reset','dress','confident']]],model:'For math conversions, we would master metric prefixes—kilo for 1,000, centi for one-hundredth, milli for one-thousandth and micro for one-millionth—so that 1 kg equals 1,000 g, 1 g equals 1,000 mg, 1 L equals 1,000 mL and 1 cc equals 1 mL. We would memorize household equivalents: 1 kg equals 2.2 lb, 1 inch equals 2.54 cm, 1 teaspoon equals 5 mL, 1 tablespoon equals 15 mL, 1 fluid ounce equals 30 mL and 1 cup equals 240 mL, dividing pounds by 2.2 to get kilograms, so 154 lb equals 70 kg. For temperature, we would use °C = (°F − 32) × 5/9 and °F = (°C × 9/5) + 32 and memorize anchors such as 98.6°F = 37°C and 100.4°F = 38°C. For the 24-hour clock, we would add 12 to afternoon hours, so 6:30 p.m. is 1830, and write midnight as 0000. For the Round One test of 50 items in 60 minutes, each of us would pace at about 72 seconds per item without verbal time announcements, answer easy items first, watch for “except” and “not,” eliminate wrong choices and answer every item, because our team average determines our seed. On match day, we would dress professionally, test our buzzers, note our letter, stay silent during the first 8 minutes, communicate softly only in the final 2 minutes, reset quickly after misses and buzz when confident.'},sources:[RULES,MAG]};
